WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.510
CHET HUSK: Hey, folks, welcome
to Microsoft Build 2026,

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:04.740
and welcome, especially,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.840
to a survey of.NET 11 runtime
libraries and SDK improvements.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.390
I'm Chet Husk, and I'm a
Product Manager on the SDK

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.230
and MSBuild teams and
here with me today is --

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.030
RICHARD LANDER: Yeah,
I'm Rich Lander.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.570
I'm also a Product Manager on
the.NET team, and I'm going

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.760
to be talking about
runtime and libraries.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.360
CHET HUSK: Awesome.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.240
We've got a great packed
flow for you today.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.250
We're going to start by
looking at some of the SDK

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.830
and tooling level
improvements and initiatives

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.080
in the.NET 11 timeframe.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.570
Then we'll yield over to Rich
to go a little bit lower

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.150
in the stack, because all the
stuff we're going to show you

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.400
at the tooling layer
builds on the work that he

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.350
and his teams have been doing.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.390
With no further ado,
let's step right in.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.450
The.NET SDK has three
major work streams

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.840
that we've been working on
for the.NET 11 releases.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.860
The first is new capabilities
and new user experiences.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.840
The second is a strong focus

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.600
on performance all the
way through the stack.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.200
The third is sort of lightening
the load of acquisition

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.540
and management of our tools.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.820
We've got some top-level notes
here, but we'll go into a lot

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.910
of these in more detail, as
well, after some of the framing

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.460
of why we think
these are important

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.810
and who these units
of work are for.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.100
Some of the things
that we'll be looking

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.650
at are net-new CLI features as
well as changes to features

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.280
that are well used today
to make them more workable

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.480
or more efficient
in an LLM context.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.140
Performance is a focus
for us because it's --

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.360
with the rise of
agents and the sort

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.300
of accelerated inner loop
requirements that we have

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.900
for these repeated multiagent
scenarios, we want to make sure

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.960
that every command you run
is as fast as possible.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.320
Let's dive right into
some examples of the work

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.020
that we've been doing on UX.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.080
For years now, dotnet run has
been sort of the backbone

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.050
of the inner loop when you're
working on your projects,

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.600
you would "dotnet run" a
project and it would run,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:16.020
and in.NET 10, we added ways
to expand how projects could,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.790
you know, "hook" how they run.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.420
This led to enhancements
around running Azure functions

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.760
in the emulator, for example.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.980
Well, one area that
hadn't received quite

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.940
as much love here was MAUI
and device specific running

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.460
of applications on your devices.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.860
When you run a MAUI
application, you typically have

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.920
to not just build the product,
but also build some kind

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.240
of an installation payload, push
it to a device, and then run

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.580
that payload on that device.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.260
Well, starting in.NET 11,

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.680
the MAUI team has contributed
enhancements to dotnet run

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.310
to make device flows
a first-class citizen

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.200
in the dotnet run experience.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.660
They've also expanded
the.NET MAUI targets

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.830
so that run actually handles
that device deployment.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.050
As you can see in the image
that we're looking at here,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.530
we're seeing an application
that is using dotnet run

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.160
to pick the target framework
it wants to deploy to,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.610
pick a device that is valid
for that target framework,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.580
and then upload the device
payload to that device

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.070
and launch it on that simulator.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.900
This is a really
powerful example

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.770
of just making common workflows
something that is part

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.400
of the first-class knowledge
that dotnet run has.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.930
What's important about this is

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.180
that run also now
understands how

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.150
to query the devices
you have available,

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.240
and this entire protocol is
something that's extensible.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.490
It works not just for MAUI,
but it could be extended

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.500
to support Uno or
Avalonia or any

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.990
of your favorite UI frameworks.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.790
So that was a really
exciting contribution

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.940
that we really thank
the MAUI team for.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.230
Moving on, we want to make sure
that the dotnet CLI works nicely

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.600
with agents of all kinds.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.860
To this end, we put work into
the dotnet CLI to make it aware

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.940
of when it is being run
under an agent context.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.390
This is necessarily kind of
a heuristic based approach,

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.010
but once we have this knowledge,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.670
this lets us do two important
categories of work.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.350
It lets us change the way
that we render output

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.200
to the output streams
that are available to us,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.550
and it helps us understand
more about who is driving us

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.100
and their usage patterns
so that we can change our

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.720
internal behavior.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.510
Some examples of this are
that the terminal logger,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.170
which is the default build
display starting in.NET 10,

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.460
has a static section where your
project outputs are emitted

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.000
and then a dynamic portion
where it's constantly updating

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.280
and showing what
targets are being built

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.000
for each project
that is being built.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.450
Well, LLMs interpret
that live portion

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.830
as a very token inefficient
stream of constant changes.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.830
Armed with this new
knowledge that the CLI has,

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:04.010
we have been able to
disable that live update

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.710
when we are run in
an LLM context.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:10.220
This is a very simple
example, but the core idea

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.610
of understanding when we are
in an LLM context and using

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.280
that to change behavior is
something that we're taking

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.620
to heart throughout
the entire dotnet CLI.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.600
In addition, we are introducing
and exploring new concepts

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.210
that are more common
thanks to agents.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.850
These days, it's more
common for agents

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.780
to spawn off separate worktrees,
which are a Git technique

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.220
for creating shallow clones of
a repo at different branches

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:41.010
so that work can be
done in parallel.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.170
However, when you
run dotnet build,

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.800
that operation is
not normally aware

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.020
of any other build that's
going on in the machine,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.110
and this can lead to significant
amounts of resource tension

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:55.080
on your machine -- or
contention rather.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.890
We're looking at strategies like
a sort of central gatekeeper

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.960
for MSBuild that all
builds have to route

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.940
through that can
delegate resources

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.590
to each individual build,

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.220
so that your overall
machine remains responsive.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.560
This is an example of
something that was kind

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.710
of historically always a
weakness in our tooling.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.880
It was very one-shot focused,
but with the rise of agents

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.730
and the rise of parallel
work on a single machine,

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.490
it's become much more
necessary that we take steps

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.070
to address this scenario.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.800
This category of work is
something you should expect

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.550
to see more of in the
dotnet CLI overall.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.520
When you do choose to do work
with the dotnet CLI, though,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.220
we want that work to be fast.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.510
We are starting on a journey

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:43.260
to make the dotnet CLI a
NativeAOT application.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.270
I'm incredibly excited for this.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.750
Some of the recent milestones
that we've hit here are

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.150
that many of these
sort of standalone

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.360
but bundled tools you
use in the dotnet CLI,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.550
like the User
Secrets, dev certs,

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:01.140
and JSON Web token
services are NativeAOT

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.090
as of two weeks ago now.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.940
Starting in Preview
6, you'll see this.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.610
The net result of this
is improved performance

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.620
and responsiveness
for these commands.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.620
We have some example numbers
here for.NET User Secrets

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:17.100
that Damian Edwards
and I took a week ago.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.130
On average, on his Windows
machine,.NET User Secrets,

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:24.270
the AOT form took about
54 milliseconds to run.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:28.770
Of that, only 14 milliseconds
was the actual application.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:34.050
The rest was the managed
dotnet CLI overhead there.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.360
We'll talk about that
more in just a second.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.730
Why are we doing
this NativeAOT push?

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.610
Well, first off, it's because
the dotnet CLI is used

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.970
so frequently that any
amount of performance

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:46.080
that we can contribute

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.670
to its shared base
multiplies incredibly well,

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.210
so even shaving 100 milliseconds
off of every command translates

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.400
to real benefits
given the number

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.630
of times each individual
developer issues a command.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.630
Also, it's an
ecosystem play for us.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.480
Many of the tools that we
make depend on libraries

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.130
that we also make, and some

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.300
of those libraries were
not AOT- or trim-friendly.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.560
By setting a milestone

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.280
of the dotnet CLI being an AOT
application, that forced us

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.520
to investigate these technical
and process blockers

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.410
and start knocking them down.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:26.310
Just over the course of the.NET
11 development cycle so far,

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.730
we've been able to make really
common libraries like templating

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:34.200
and NuGet and even parts of
MSBuild more trim-friendly.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.180
This is an ongoing effort,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.780
and not all use cases
are trim-friendly yet,

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:43.050
but it is kind of the North
Star for the overall effort.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:46.620
The hope is that especially with
some of these NuGet libraries,

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.920
unblocking these core
Microsoft provided libraries

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.380
from being trim friendly
makes it possible

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.650
for the entire ecosystem
to make trimmed

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:59.910
and AOT applications
more effectively.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.570
That's just some of the bundled
tools, but the big thing here,

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.880
the big news, is that
the entire dotnet CLI is

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.450
on its arc to be AOT as well.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.970
Some of the technical blockers
I mentioned, I've touched

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:12.900
on already, but some

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:17.460
of the other ones were
complete replacements.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.980
We had telemetry
libraries, for example,

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.320
that we had to migrate
to OpenTelemetry.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.450
We had old com patterns that
needed to be made trim friendly.

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:29.510
A lot of this was stuff that the
runtime had already provided,

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:32.210
and we just had not
taken advantage yet.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:34.550
We're hopeful that the
dotnet CLI taking advantage

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.210
of those will start to lead
to, sort of, real benefits

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:44.400
to our end users, as well as
championing NativeAOT as a valid

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.310
and awesome technology
in its own right.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:49.200
An example of some of the
wins we get from this,

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.130
you can see in this Aspire
Dashboard trace below --

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:54.090
that's right.

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:56.370
Now that the dotnet
CLI emits OTEL,

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.590
you can use the Aspire
Dashboard and all

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:02.730
of the Aspire tools you're
familiar with to trace it.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.040
In this display
that you're seeing,

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.230
the top five spans all come
from the native portion

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:13.590
of the dotnet CLI now,
and the bottom five come

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.800
from the managed portion
of the dotnet CLI.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.740
Over time, we will be moving
more and more commands

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.120
into the native space as
their technical blockers are

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.360
eliminated and as
our resources allow.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.460
We're trying to
optimize commands

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.750
that are both dependency
free, relatively speaking,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.060
as well as frequently used
by the community to kind

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.460
of maximize the benefit there.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:39.450
Right now, we're looking

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.480
at the.NET solution management
commands, but other commands

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:45.900
that are high up on my personal
list are template related

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.810
and tool management related.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:53.190
This leads us to a place where
we might even have scenarios

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:55.920
like the DNX command
being a standalone

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.320
NativeAOT application.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.450
It's a really exciting
step for us, and it opens

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.070
up a whole bunch
of opportunities

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:06.510
that we'll hopefully be talking
about later in future releases.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.420
We are hitting some
more highlights here.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.110
We're embracing threads
in MSBuild, in.NET 11,

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.420
and not just processes.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:19.110
Today's MSBuild uses a
multi-process architecture

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:23.850
that is great for isolation and
safety but has high overheads

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.600
in terms of inter-process
communication costs in things

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.630
like CoreCLR JIT loading.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.520
This multi-threaded mode is
something we've been working

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.370
on for a while, and it's
not ready to use today,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:39.150
but it will be ready to use
by the time.NET 11 launches.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:42.390
Look for more
announcements there.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:44.760
If you are a task author,
you can follow the link

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.230
on this page,
aka.ms/msbuild/mt-tasks,

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:51.570
to learn more about the
changes you would need to make

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:54.270
to your task to adopt
to this ecosystem.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:58.500
We think it is very promising
in terms of performance here

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.440
and opens up a whole bunch
of additional optimizations

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:05.130
that we can layer on top, but,
again, look for more on this

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:09.330
as we get better measurement
numbers to share with you all.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:10.890
The final sort of
pillar we wanted

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.450
to tackle was Acquisition,

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.150
and for this we have two main
things we've been working on.

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.210
The first is a NativeAOT
tool called dotnet up,

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:22.200
which we hope will give users
who work on multiple platforms,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.740
or across team members that
work on multiple platforms,

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.120
a consistent way to acquire and
manage their.NET toolchains.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.490
It does all the things that
you would hope it would,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:35.370
support nightlies, use
global.json if it's there,

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.790
and it does this using
user-level installs

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.460
of the tooling, so
no admin required.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:44.430
There will be previews of
this tool coming soon,

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:45.870
and that's like
the Blizzard-style

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:48.180
"soon" if you're a gamer.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.910
I did a dedicated recording
of this for Build.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:54.120
That should be up now, as well,
so feel free to check that out

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:55.980
if you want a deep
dive on how it works

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:58.770
and to take a look at it.

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:01.140
Finally, the other arc of
Acquisition we've been looking

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.900
at is how you download
the SDK today,

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:07.200
and what you get when
you download it.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.310
The SDK is downloaded many,
many times for many reasons,

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:13.680
not just for end users,
but also, importantly,

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.220
for containers, which
are a primary way

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.010
that people build their
applications these days.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:23.970
To that end, we were looking at
ways to reduce the on-disk size

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:25.890
of the SDK, and we discovered

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:30.240
that tarballs support
what's called hard links,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:34.350
which are ways of pointing
-- well, it's like symlinks.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:37.230
It's pointing to files that
share the exact same content

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:39.390
to one canonical resource.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:42.960
We found a way to inspect
the layouts of the SDK

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.570
that we make every time
we produce our tarballs

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.380
and our packages of all kinds
and do an automated unification

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:53.430
and deduplication, and that has
yielded incredible results.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:57.480
Over the course of the.NET
11 previews, so far,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:01.410
we have shaved quite
a few megabytes --

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.720
somewhere on the order
of 80-ish megabytes --

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.840
for all these different
platforms, and more,

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.390
from all of the SDK payloads.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.250
This is huge.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:13.950
A smaller payload is
faster to get to you,

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.410
and it's also just less
overall network IO,

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:19.560
especially for those SDK
containers that I mentioned.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.180
This is an example
of us building

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.900
on the runtime's better
knowledge of tarballs starting

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:28.080
in.NET 7, building some
confidence with tarballs

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.380
through the SDK containerization
feature, and then applying

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:36.540
that tarball confidence to our
own packaging to deliver wins

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.930
for our users and our customers.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.730
I think that's a great
segue to start talking

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:44.070
about the runtime here because
everything I just showed you

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:46.530
would not be possible
without the work that Rich

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:48.000
and his teams have done.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.520
Rich, you want to take us
through what's coming in 11?

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:51.090
RICHARD LANDER: Yes.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:52.860
Yeah, I love the tarball piece.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.380
You are very familiar
with those APIs.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:58.710
I think you're our best user.

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:02.130
Okay. Yeah, I want to talk

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:07.080
about what's been happening
in the libraries.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:12.120
In Preview 4, we announced a
big update to the process API.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.620
You typically use this
API to start a process,

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.620
capture its results,
things like that.

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:23.610
Unfortunately, this is
actually somewhat difficult

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:28.230
and has been a long
source of bugs for users,

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:33.180
so we now have a set of,
sort of, convenience APIs

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:37.890
that we expose that do the
sorts of things that you want,

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:41.190
do them correctly,
and do it fast.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.770
I'm going to quickly
go through those.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:47.520
The first one -- yeah,
on the next slide.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.360
The first one is, oh, right,
this is actually the bad case.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.620
We're going to go
over that first.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:58.980
In this particular kind of
somewhat made-up scenario,

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:02.790
we're going to call "dotnet
build" with full diagnostics.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.100
We're going to have
both standard error

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:08.070
and standard output,
like, you know --

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:10.740
yeah, both of those present.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:15.780
The key point here is the two
lines that end in "read to end."

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.840
What actually happens, and
this is, quote/unquote,

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:23.640
"the bad thing," is there
are buffers attached

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.040
to both standard output
and standard error.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:29.700
What happens is the code
says, "Read standard output

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:32.310
to the end before
reading anything

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:35.530
from the standard error stream."

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:38.610
What happens is standard error,

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:41.170
the buffer for that,
gets filled up.

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:46.380
The writer, which is
basically dotnet build,

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:49.980
it stops because it can't fill
the thing up, but you're waiting

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.050
for standard error to end.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.630
This is, obviously, a deadlock,

00:16:54.630 --> 00:17:00.000
so you have to write code a
different way to avoid this.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:07.530
Now I will transition to looking
at how this works better.

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:09.120
We have a series of new APIs

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:11.130
that could do slightly
different things depending

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:12.450
on your use case.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.750
This one's called
RunAndCaptureTextAsync.

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:20.340
It gives you this object back,
this process text output object,

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:23.790
and it, basically, has
all the information

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:24.960
that you would want on it.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.720
It gives you all the standard
output, all the standard error,

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.910
and gives you the exit codes.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.640
Then you can do whatever
you want with them.

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:38.520
The next one is called
ReadAllLinesAsync.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.830
It's somewhat similar,
except it allows you

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.050
to "foreach over" all the lines.

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:45.840
Then you can ask the line if
it's standard error or not

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.720
and do whatever it is
that you would like.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:50.820
This is also, as you
can probably guess,

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.870
more of a streaming
type of model.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:18:03.150
The next one is --
this one allows you

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:05.790
to do the same thing
as, say, you know,

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:10.230
the example here is dotnet
help, pipe to grep.

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.660
So if you're working
on the shell,

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:14.130
you might pipe all the time.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:19.650
I certainly do, so how do you
write the same thing in C#?

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.270
The big win here is actually

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:24.810
with this
CreateAnonymousPipe API.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:28.380
In this case, we have
a reader and a writer,

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:31.290
or sorry, read and write sides.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.680
Then we pass the write side
to the standard output

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:38.850
of the first command, and
then we pass the read

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:42.090
to the standard input
of the second command.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:43.500
It makes sense, right?

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:46.530
Then we just start
the two processes,

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:48.780
and exactly the
right thing happens.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:51.600
This is the entirety of
the code that you need.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.570
Then we just wait for the
second command to exit,

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:56.520
and then everything is --

00:18:56.520 --> 00:19:00.820
all the resources are
released correctly.

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:05.100
Next one is a
fire-and-forget story.

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:07.920
Sometimes you just want
to start a process

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:11.310
and just have it do its thing,

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:15.720
have it release resources
correctly, and not do anything.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:19.230
In this case, the definition
of fire-and-forget is

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:24.930
that you're not trying to find
out what the results were.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:26.190
This does this.

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:31.800
The only interesting nuance is
you can either have the process

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:37.030
killed on parent exit or not,
depending on your use case.

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.660
Chet spent a fair
bit of time talking

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.780
about NativeAOT and trimming.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:48.060
There's actually a separate
API, a separate type,

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:51.790
called SafeProcessHandle.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:58.980
If trimming is something
you're super-concerned about,

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:01.590
then you can use
this type instead.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:08.470
We've done work so that it trims
better than the process type.

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:15.760
Let's switch gears and talk
about text processing.

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:24.180
Much of this is about
Unicode conformance.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:28.170
We now have two pairs of
APIs for utf8 and utf16.

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:31.010
One is called IsValid, and
the other one is called

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:34.220
IndexOfInvalidSubsequence.

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:39.680
Where this is useful is, say,
if you're getting Unicode text

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:43.320
from a file or over the
wire, and you're wanting

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.770
to just validate that it's
valid, and then if it's not,

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:50.160
know exactly where
the bad code starts,

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:51.840
that's what this API does.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.270
It allows you to produce
great error messages

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:55.470
or have good diagnostics

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.810
or whatever it is
that you're doing.

00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:00.690
Another one is if you've
done any text processing,

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.840
particularly that comes from a
variety of operating systems,

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:08.580
you'll know that new line
measurement is quite a pain,

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:12.030
so there's a new regex
option called AnyNewLine.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:15.210
It works like with r or n.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:20.910
I think it's got -- it has
several new line variants

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:25.560
that could be in the text
that you are processing,

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.990
so that's incredibly useful.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:36.450
Last is we've made some of the
string methods Rune aware.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:40.450
Just a quick take on
that if you're unaware,

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:47.550
say you're using utf16
strings, and it's, like, oh,

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:52.470
I've got these char
values, so clearly,

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:57.570
this can hold any Unicode
quote/unquote "character,"

00:21:57.570 --> 00:21:59.820
and we're all good to go.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.210
Well, it doesn't
actually work that way.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:09.420
Lots of Unicode characters fit
within 2 bytes, within 16 bits,

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:10.920
but many actually don't.

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:12.930
The emojis are the kind

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:16.710
of poster children
of ones that don't.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:21.900
They can take multiple
characters, two or more.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:27.330
Rune really describes
a Unicode code point.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:28.500
It could be a character.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:32.830
It could be a letter,
a number, or an emoji.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:38.190
These APIs now work
correctly with those.

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:42.690
I think I have a demo, a
quick bit of code, for that.

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:44.880
It's a little bit
of an eye chart.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:50.670
What this is showing is, say
I want to do some analysis

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:55.170
of Chet's comments on GitHub.

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:58.110
I think Chet is an
amazing code reviewer

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:04.500
and is often telling people,
"Looks good to me, ship it."

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:07.590
but sometimes Chet
does this with emojis.

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:12.120
This code, basically, what
it's doing is it's saying, oh,

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:15.750
I'm going to break all this text

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:20.520
into new lines using the
Regex new line capability.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:25.860
Then I am going to "foreach
over" all of these lines

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:30.810
and check to see if
Chet specified any one

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:36.180
of these ship-it
style declarations.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:38.670
Then I'm going to print
the context before

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:41.250
and after this, this content.

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:47.010
I can see what Chet said for
context, and then I'm going

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:50.790
to follow up where I think
he made the wrong call.

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:54.900
So, yeah, that's super-useful.

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:58.890
Just for one bit of clarity,

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:00.570
if I was to really
write this thing,

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:03.270
I'm not sure I would actually
use these particular APIs.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.520
I might actually use
the search values APIs,

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:09.810
which I'm a big fan of, but I
thought this was a good way

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.040
of showing these APIs.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:15.030
Okay. Let's move on.

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:20.010
System.text.Json, we've been
working on this component for,

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:22.590
I don't know, six or seven
years or something like that.

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:26.190
We keep on investing
in it, which is great.

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:29.880
One of the places, and I
actually see this with agents,

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:35.220
as well, is you want all this
policy kind of in your --

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.920
for your serializer to say what
it is that you want to happen.

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:39.840
It's usually about naming

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:46.080
or not emitting a particular
property if it's null.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:48.990
Let's look at the code.

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:52.860
In this particular
case, we're using --

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:56.790
we're able to specify for a
whole type for event data

00:24:56.790 --> 00:25:00.630
that we don't want to write any
properties when they're null

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:02.100
for the entirety of the type.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:05.430
That's super useful, and then
if we go down to the bottom

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:07.230
or the middle, actually,

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:11.310
we can see that we're setting
adjacent serializer options,

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:18.900
which says that we want to use
Pascal case for serializing.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:22.530
Then you can see that when
we actually do serialize

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:24.060
at the bottom, we
use that option,

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:27.300
so then Pascal case will
obviously be our default.

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:29.010
Then if you go back
up to the top,

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:32.790
you'll see that for event
name, we have an override,

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:38.760
which says that we want
to use camel case.

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:41.430
So that's a new capability

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:45.720
that gives you a little bit more
control while still enabling all

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:46.950
the convenience.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:54.030
Okay. I'm actually a
big fan of JSON Lines.

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:57.450
I've been using it for
a number of years.

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:02.520
What JSON Lines is, is one
JSON document per line.

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:06.300
There's another variant
called NDJSON, which is --

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:07.260
I think that's what it's called.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.520
It's very, very similar.

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:16.680
Actually, if you look at certain
agents, they have these logs

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:20.070
of the conversations, then
they're often as JSON Lines.

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.000
There's some scenarios

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:24.870
where JSON Lines is
absolutely the right choice.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:27.480
You can see in this example,

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:33.570
it's showing how we can async
write the set of JSON documents

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:40.650
that are produced by an
IAsyncEnumerable method,

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.750
and the way to opt
into this is with this

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:45.970
"top-level values is true."

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:57.240
Just super quick, we did a
bunch of work on compression.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.270
I'm not going to go
through all of these.

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:01.740
Perhaps the biggest change is

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:06.000
that Zstandard APIs are now
integrated into the product,

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:10.140
but we made improvements in
several different places

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:14.010
for several different
algorithms.

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:16.980
Yeah, this is all
continues to be important.

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:19.800
I think I have a
quick chart on this.

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:27.510
Yes, so just for kicks,
I compressed the system,

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:34.620
I/O compression C# files,
in the dotnet runtime repo.

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:39.450
You can see I was trying to
be a bit meta, and this is --

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:41.370
just a massive caveat.

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:44.400
This is one example.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:45.780
This is only text.

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:48.720
It's not it's not some
other type of artifact,

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:51.030
and it's only one body of text,

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:56.700
but I thought it was just
interesting, so I compressed

00:27:56.700 --> 00:28:01.230
with both the optimal setting,
but then, also a smaller size,

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:04.350
which is different than optimal.

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:08.490
You can see for this
particular body of text,

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:13.950
brotli came out best with
Zstandard being very, very close

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:16.390
and quite competitive.

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:19.620
I think it was like -- well,
it doesn't matter how big,

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:21.960
but I think it was
like 15 megabytes.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:23.220
No, it can't have
been that much.

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:27.780
I actually don't remember how
large the source text was,

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:33.360
but this was this was great, and
you can now do this at home.

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:38.160
Okay. Now we're going
to switch gears a lot.

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:44.820
I'm going to talk about two
enormous runtime projects

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:49.050
that we've been working on, so
the first time is Runtime Async.

00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:51.930
I'm going to give you a
quick introduction first.

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:55.380
I think everyone knows that
Async code is pervasive in C#.

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:59.280
It's pervasive in
many languages.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.350
Runtime Async, you
should think of it

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:03.510
as a new optimization
for Async Code.

00:29:03.510 --> 00:29:05.910
It's not some new model.

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:09.300
There are no new keywords
that you need to learn

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:10.290
or anything like that.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:12.600
Yeah, no source
changes required.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:16.140
It's really just a fast path
that's decided at compile time

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:19.840
when you compile your
code with dotnet build.

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:24.960
Yeah, and it's
completely compatible.

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:26.910
You can enable Runtime Async.

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:29.620
You can call old code.

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:33.120
Old code can call new code.

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:37.680
There's no compatibility
break in any direction.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.230
The big takeaway is when
you write new code --

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:43.200
and by "new code," I mean,
when you enable Runtime Async,

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:44.700
there's no state machines.

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:47.250
We'll talk a little bit
more about what that means.

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:50.730
Opt-in for.NET 11, will
likely be the default

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:54.390
and the new normal in.NET 12.

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:58.890
But again, even in.NET 12,
you'll still be able to --

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:02.460
yeah, there's no
compatibility break ever.

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:03.330
Even when it's the default,

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:05.070
you can still do whatever
you wanted before.

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:08.580
Okay. The opt in is super easy.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:09.990
We have this features property.

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:15.130
You just type "runtime-async
equals on," and that's it.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:21.240
Just a quick way to
think about this,

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:24.510
so these names are not perfect,

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:26.790
but these are the
names we're using.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:29.250
One is we call Compiler Async,

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:31.620
meaning that's the
traditional model that,

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:35.170
say, the C# compiler uses.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:38.340
The source compiler
owns the state machine,

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:39.600
so you have an async method

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:42.540
that gets rewritten
as a state machine.

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:47.370
Then it has a certain
set of mechanics,

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:52.380
that I'm largely not going to
get into, that are executed

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:55.080
by nature of how this
code is written.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.170
With Runtime Async, the
code is not rewritten.

00:30:58.170 --> 00:31:03.240
The state machine does
not exist in your.dll

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:05.760
or.exe that you generate.

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:08.250
The runtime takes
care of all of that,

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:11.550
so we still have
suspension and resumption.

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:16.290
All of this machinery is
owned by the runtime.

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:19.260
We basically have many state
machines that get boiled

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:23.190
down to one runtime
managed system.

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:29.100
Just one, it's kind of a
slight joke, is, like I said,

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:32.100
Compiler Async is the
language compiler model

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:35.340
and Runtime Async
is the new one.

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:38.430
The thing is, is that it's
almost all implemented

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:43.560
in the JIT, which is
itself a compiler,

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:45.210
which we'll see a
little bit more.

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:47.640
They're both Compiler Async
of a kind, but anyway,

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:49.840
we're still using these names.

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:54.990
I'm sure that served to
just confuse people.

00:31:54.990 --> 00:31:58.680
In terms of the benefits,
why did we do this project?

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:01.110
So cleaner stack frames for --

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:04.080
largely for production
diagnostics, there's a small win

00:32:04.080 --> 00:32:07.140
on size for the binaries.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:09.960
A large portion of the
motivation was perf.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:13.890
We think we can make Async
Code faster, particularly

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:17.290
when all the code
is runtime async.

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.300
Once people start adopting
this, we'll encourage them

00:32:21.300 --> 00:32:24.570
to convert their whole
stack to Runtime Async.

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:27.270
All that means is just
turn Runtime Async

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:29.910
on for all your projects.

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:32.640
Like I said, that'll
eventually be default,

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:36.990
and there's potential for
Async code to actually improve

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:39.480
over time without
recompiling your code.

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:43.980
So imagine, in the future,
you've got a bunch of --

00:32:43.980 --> 00:32:47.070
you say you have your
NuGet feed for, you know,

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:51.570
your company's code
and you upgrade.NET 13

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:54.750
and you haven't actually
recompiled that code,

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:57.330
although it is using
Runtime Async,

00:32:57.330 --> 00:32:59.880
there could be improvements
to the Async infrastructure

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:02.940
within the runtime, and then
your code would just get faster

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:05.430
because all that code is
in the runtime and not

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:07.680
in your library anymore.

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:09.100
Okay.

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:15.790
Yeah, so here's a fun
chart that I made.

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:21.900
We turned on runtime async
for the product in Preview 4.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:24.910
That's the one we
released in May.

00:33:26.730 --> 00:33:30.750
That means it was not on in
Preview 3, and so I went

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:34.230
through the product and
found some heavy hitters.

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:38.760
You can see that the product
basically got smaller

00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:42.060
in Preview 4, and that
can be attributed largely

00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:46.530
to the removal of
the state machines.

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:49.320
These libraries all
had state machines

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:51.490
in them before and
now they don't.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:56.040
Here's a sample app.

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:58.110
We're not going to go into
detail on what it does,

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:01.230
but it's basically just trying
-- it's got outer async,

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:03.810
middle async and inner
async, and it's just trying

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:09.000
to create kind of
interleaved nested async.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:11.730
One bit of clarification
is I intentionally used

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:13.890
Task.CompletedTask,
which is, obviously,

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:16.140
not normally how you
would write your code.

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:19.710
The reason is, is I wanted the
stack frames to just be purely

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:22.340
about the Async machinery.

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:25.910
I wanted it to not be about what
the code is actually doing,

00:34:25.910 --> 00:34:32.130
so that you could just see
the most-bare stack frames.

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:34.680
Then one other piece of
information that you need

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:38.880
to know is that -- so inner
async is obviously the

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:40.590
most nested.

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:45.900
In that one, we write,
"Environment.StackTrace,"

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:47.640
so you can see the most nested.

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:53.160
Then in middle async after
an inner async is called,

00:34:53.160 --> 00:34:57.000
I throw an exception in.

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:01.470
Just for some reason, I wanted
them to be in different frames.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:04.030
Okay. Let's see what happens.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:10.050
In.NET 10, what we see is, yeah,

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:12.120
the first part is
the stack trace,

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:15.570
and you immediately
will see, oh, yeah,

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:18.060
I see these state machines
that Rich is talking about.

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:19.890
They're definitely there.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:24.810
It's kind of a bit messy, but
this is -- it's also accurate.

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:31.200
It's perfect fidelity of what
your call stack looks like.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:37.430
Then you can see at the bottom
we have Exception.ToString,

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.170
and it's cleaned up.

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:42.210
We had already cleaned this up.

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:44.430
It's basically --
the distinction is,

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:48.150
is that Exception.ToString
is supposed to kind

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:52.470
of deliver you the intent
so that you can reason

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:59.760
about what your app was actually
doing, but the stack trace,

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:01.950
like Environment.StackTrace,
is always intended

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:05.700
to be perfectly accurate.

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:09.030
Let's look at
the.NET 11 example.

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:12.750
Now you see that they're
basically the same.

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:16.290
Environment.StackTrace
now is just as accurate

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:26.440
as it was before, but those
state machine frames are gone.

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:33.960
What I did on the next
slide is I, basically,

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:37.780
overlaid the two
slides on one another.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:43.380
You can see the frames
that have been removed.

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:45.950
It's basically you
have inner async.

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:48.770
We then basically start
the state machine.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:52.130
Then we come back
into inner async.

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:54.590
With these state machines,
you come back multiple times

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:57.530
if there were multiple
awaits in it.

00:36:57.530 --> 00:37:01.530
Okay. Now, let's -- all right.

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:04.170
I want to talk about NativeAOT.

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:09.510
The main point is that, yeah,

00:37:09.510 --> 00:37:12.240
NativeAOT works
perfectly with this.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:17.750
NativeAOT doesn't really have a
runtime, so it's just relying

00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:22.970
on NativeAOT users,
RyuJIT, our JIT compiler

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:25.170
as its code generator.

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:30.450
It just -- all of the
system works perfectly well

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:33.360
with NativeAOT.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:35.580
Basically, there's
nothing to say here

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:38.760
because the whole system
works perfectly with AOT just

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:40.440
because of the way our
architecture works,

00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:43.330
so that's great.

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:48.190
Now I want to talk
about memory safety,

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:52.950
so this is a massive
project that we started.

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:56.250
It's a two-release project,
11 and 12, to some degree,

00:37:56.250 --> 00:37:58.410
a bit like Runtime Async.

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:00.540
Although Runtime Async
will actually be supported

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:05.880
in.NET 11, just not enabled by
default, but memory safety,

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:09.330
it's in preview in.NET 11.

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:12.570
Right now, we're defining
a set of language changes.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:15.810
I actually wrote a blog post
on this like a week or so ago.

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:18.720
It goes into much more
detail on what --

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:21.660
yeah, goes into
much more detail.

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:23.250
Yeah, so this release,

00:38:23.250 --> 00:38:25.980
we're defining a set
of language changes.

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:29.610
We're applying those changes
to System.Private.CoreLib,

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:32.340
which is the bottom-most
managed library,

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:38.070
and we're also making a bunch
of changes so that ourselves

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:41.610
and anyone else needs
to use Unsafe less.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.890
We want to reduce
the need for Unsafe.

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:48.330
Then in.NET 12, we're basically
just finishing everything off.

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:51.050
The takeaway, which the
blog post gets into,

00:38:51.050 --> 00:38:56.250
is that we're redesigning
the Unsafe keywords

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:59.760
to mean a reviewable
caller contract as opposed

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:04.950
to establishing an
Unsafe context.

00:39:04.950 --> 00:39:08.340
APIs like Unsafe and
MemoryMarshal, they'll be marked

00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:12.880
with this contract,
yeah, when we're done.

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:17.880
There's a ton of work
that's been going

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:22.710
on to make certain patterns
higher performance,

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:27.070
and a lot of this has to do
with bounds-check elimination

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:32.910
or making inlining work better.

00:39:32.910 --> 00:39:35.810
These are cases where we

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:38.390
and others would often
write Unsafe code

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:42.500
because the JIT wasn't able
to quite do the right thing.

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:44.600
In these cases, we're not
doing the right thing,

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:47.040
so I'll show you some examples.

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:51.600
I like this one, so
in the second line,

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:57.570
you see this check that's
saying, "only continue

00:39:57.570 --> 00:40:03.330
if I plus 2 is less than
length," so that we know

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:07.320
for sure that the
next two indexes

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:10.470
on the particular
span will be safe.

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:16.110
The JITs in.NET 10 and
before, it actually --

00:40:16.110 --> 00:40:19.860
that check works
correctly with I plus 2

00:40:19.860 --> 00:40:23.580
because the check matches I plus
2, so we would only be able

00:40:23.580 --> 00:40:26.310
to reason about if the checks --

00:40:26.310 --> 00:40:30.750
sorry, if the check and the
index perfectly matched.

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:35.850
It would actually still do a
bounds check for I plus 1.

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:43.140
Now it realizes that I plus 1 is
within the range of the check

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:48.000
as I plus 2, and so now
this code gets faster.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:54.300
The next one is very, very
similar, except it's just --

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:57.210
the polarity of it changes
a little bit, so this one,

00:40:57.210 --> 00:41:01.050
we're saying, basically,

00:41:01.050 --> 00:41:05.550
does the span go past
the size of an int?

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:08.730
Is it at least 4 bytes?

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:12.110
Then you can see then
we do this span.Slice

00:41:12.110 --> 00:41:17.420
that is the same size, but we're
coming at it from the end.

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:19.280
The JIT previously
didn't work well

00:41:19.280 --> 00:41:24.780
with this tail slice
pattern, so now it does.

00:41:28.700 --> 00:41:30.620
This is an inlining one.

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:37.920
In the past, what would happen
is GetLength would get inlined

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:39.000
into the demo.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:42.090
Then we would have these
two null checks on s,

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:44.970
and they would both be run.

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:51.180
In addition, the "throw new
ArgumentNullException" that,

00:41:51.180 --> 00:41:56.700
sometimes, when you have an
actual throw in the stack frame,

00:41:56.700 --> 00:42:00.510
and yes, then that can
pessimize the code as well.

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:05.100
In this particular case, when
we inline, we're only left

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:12.600
with the s is null return minus
1, cleaner code, less code.

00:42:12.600 --> 00:42:14.590
This runs faster.

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:22.230
Those are all cool, but they
don't necessarily completely

00:42:22.230 --> 00:42:26.700
change the game, so we've
kind of taken this work

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:30.030
and applied it to SIMD.

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:33.900
SIMD stands for Single
Instruction/Multiple Data,

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:36.670
quote/unquote "vector
instructions."

00:42:38.730 --> 00:42:42.720
These get used extremely
pervasively across the product,

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:46.470
and so it relies on a
bunch of Unsafe code.

00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:49.320
If we could make
SIMD memory safe,

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:52.770
then we would just be removing
a whole category of Unsafe code

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:55.140
from the product, which
would be excellent.

00:42:55.140 --> 00:43:01.350
We'll look at an example of
what the code looks like today.

00:43:01.350 --> 00:43:03.270
I'm not going to go
through this line by line.

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:07.080
The takeaway is, okay,
count the MemoryMarshals

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:09.630
and Unsafe calls in
this code block.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:13.600
You're, like, okay, yeah,
it's probably not safe.

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:18.750
Then in the code
after, you can see

00:43:18.750 --> 00:43:21.630
that all those API
calls are gone.

00:43:21.630 --> 00:43:25.830
The JIT is able to do the right
thing, basically, in part,

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:28.530
by relying on some
of those changes

00:43:28.530 --> 00:43:30.920
that I showed you before.

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:32.630
We can now deploy this code

00:43:32.630 --> 00:43:37.590
across the product,
and it gets better.

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:44.100
I hope you're installing.NET
11 previews, trying out some

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:47.670
of these new features,
giving us feedback on GitHub

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:52.320
and on blog posts, so that
we can ship the right thing.

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:55.440
Okay, awesome, Chet says yes.

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:57.630
CHET HUSK: Yes, and
definitely the feedback

00:43:57.630 --> 00:44:00.180
on the GitHub
discussions and issues.

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:01.470
The team is there.

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:03.120
We look at those
things all the time,

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:07.290
and the earlier you all get
hands on with these changes,

00:44:07.290 --> 00:44:10.680
the better they will be
for the GA this fall.

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:12.960
RICHARD LANDER: Absolutely,
and for the features

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:16.800
that we're not shipping
until.NET 12 as well.

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:18.730
Awesome. Thank you.
